<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">New Zealand Bill of Rights Act 1990</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1990">1990</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> Bill of Rights Act 1990 is a statute that sets out civil and political rights, such as freedom of expression, religion and movement, the right to vote and rights in criminal procedure. It is not entrenched and does not allow courts to strike down legislation, but laws are to be interpreted consistently with it where possible. It forms part of New Zealand's unwritten constitution.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>'s constitution rests on parliamentary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>, and since the abolition of the Legislative Council in 1951 its parliament has had a single chamber, so a government with a majority faces few checks. After the Third National Government led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-muldoon">Robert Muldoon</a>, these arrangements became a major political issue. In 1985 the Minister of Justice, Geoffrey Palmer, introduced A Bill of Rights for New Zealand: A White Paper, which proposed an entrenched bill as supreme law. The plan met widespread controversy because it would have let courts invalidate acts of parliament, and on 10 October 1989 the bill passed its first reading as an ordinary statute; The Press called it &quot;watered down&quot;.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The Act binds the legislature, the executive and the judiciary and any body performing a public function, and its rights may be limited where the limit can be demonstrably justified in a free and democratic society. They include freedom from torture and from medical experimentation without consent, the right of citizens aged 18 or over to vote and to stand for parliament, freedom of thought, conscience and religion, freedom from discrimination, the rights of ethnic, religious and linguistic minorities, protection from unreasonable search and arbitrary arrest, and fair-trial rights such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jury-trial">trial by jury</a> for offences carrying two years' imprisonment or more. The attorney-general must report to parliament whenever a bill is inconsistent with it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Because the Act lists no remedies, the courts developed them: the exclusion of evidence obtained in breach of it, now in section 30 of the Evidence Act 2006; reductions in sentence for undue delay; and compensatory damages, first awarded by the Court of Appeal in Simpson v Attorney-General. In July 2015 Justice Heath of the High Court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auckland">Auckland</a> made the first declaration of inconsistency, in Taylor v Attorney-General, holding that removing prisoners' voting rights was an unjustified limit.</p><p>The Make It 16 campaign later argued successfully before the Supreme Court that a voting age of 18 was unjustified age discrimination. Whether <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> should adopt an entrenched bill of rights has long been debated: Palmer argued for one, while the law professor James Allan argued against.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “New Zealand Bill of Rights Act 1990” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>New Zealand is a constitutional monarchy and parliamentary democracy without a single written constitution; its constitutional order rests on statutes such as the Constitution Act 1986 and the <b>New Zealand Bill of Rights Act 1990</b>, on convention and, increasingly, on the Treaty of Waitangi.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-zealand">New Zealand · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
